@using BlazorRamp.DocSite.Pages.GettingStarted
@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_CSS_Classes}/{GlobalValues.CSS_Path_Part_Position}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.CSS_Name_Position @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api_Usage</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.CSS_Name_Position @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api_Usage</h1>


<p>
    Everything you need to work with the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-position-*</span> CSS classes: the utility classes provided, the strongly-typed <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Position</span> C# object
    used to apply them, and the supporting enum used to select a position value.
</p>

<h2 class="page-heading">Overview</h2>

<p>
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Position</span> is a <b>utility class</b>, not a BEM component class like <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Section</span> or <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Button</span>.
    Rather than describing a block, element, or its variants, it sets a single CSS property - <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position</span> - to a fixed value. Utility classes are flat
    and single-purpose by design, so they can be freely combined with each other and with any other class - including component classes - on the same element.
</p>
<p>
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Section</span> also exposes its own <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Section.Position()</span> method, sharing the same <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">UnitPosition</span>
    enum. Prefer that method when the element already has <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-section</span> applied; use this utility class for any other element.
</p>

<section>
    <h2 id="c#-classes" class="page-heading">C# API</h2>
    <p>
        <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Position</span> provides a utility class that sets an element's CSS <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position</span>.
    </p>
    <section class="code-table">
        <h3 id="position-class" class="code-table__heading">Position class</h3>
        <table aria-labelledby="position-class">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Return</th>
                    <th scope="col">Static Method</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>string</td>
                    <td>Position.SetAs(UnitPosition unitPosition)</td>
                    <td>Gets the class that sets an element's CSS <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position</span>.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>
</section>

<section>
    <h2 class="page-heading">Supporting enum</h2>
    <section class="code-table">
        <h3 id="unit-position-enum" class="code-table__heading">UnitPosition enum</h3>
        <p>
            Sets a CSS <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position</span> value. Used by <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Section.Position()</span> and by the
            <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Position</span> utility class.
        </p>
        <table aria-labelledby="unit-position-enum">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Value</th>
                    <th scope="col">Name</th>
                    <th scope="col">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>0</td>
                    <td>Static</td>
                    <td>The element is positioned according to normal document flow.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>1</td>
                    <td>Relative</td>
                    <td>The element is positioned according to normal flow, then offset relative to itself if offset properties are set.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>2</td>
                    <td>Absolute</td>
                    <td>The element is removed from normal flow and positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>3</td>
                    <td>Fixed</td>
                    <td>The element is removed from normal flow and positioned relative to the viewport.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>4</td>
                    <td>Sticky</td>
                    <td>The element is positioned according to normal flow, then treated as fixed once its scrolling ancestor reaches a given scroll position.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </section>
</section>

<section class="code-table">
    <h3 id="css-classes" class="code-table__heading">CSS classes</h3>

    <table aria-labelledby="css-classes">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Class</th>
                <th scope="col">Type</th>
                <th scope="col">Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>br-position-static</td>
                <td>Utility</td>
                <td>Sets <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position: static</span>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>br-position-relative</td>
                <td>Utility</td>
                <td>Sets <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position: relative</span>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>br-position-absolute</td>
                <td>Utility</td>
                <td>Sets <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position: absolute</span>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>br-position-fixed</td>
                <td>Utility</td>
                <td>Sets <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position: fixed</span>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>br-position-sticky</td>
                <td>Utility</td>
                <td>Sets <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">position: sticky</span>.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

<section class="@Margin.BlockEnd(UnitSpace.Five)">
    <h2 class="page-heading">Usage</h2>
    <p>
        The following example uses <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Position.SetAs(UnitPosition.Sticky)</span> so the
        red box sticks when its 50px below its parent containers top.
    </p>
    
    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CssClassesSnippets.Position_Code_Example" AriaLabel="Position code example" />

    <div style="width:400px;height:200px;" class="@Overflow.Y(UnitScroll.Auto)" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable region for position sticky example.">
        <p>
            Text inside a 200px * 200px div with a scrollbar and red box below it that uses position sticky. It has a top of 50px which means the red box will never 
            move above this. Below the red box is just some lorem ipsum text for you to scroll.
        </p>
        <div style="background-color:red;height:100px;width:100px;top:50px;" class="@Position.SetAs(UnitPosition.Sticky)"></div>
        <p>
            Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Praesent facilisis lectus sit amet varius convallis. Suspendisse tempor neque sit amet neque dignissim, ac accumsan enim pellentesque. 
            Nam a nisl vitae magna lacinia placerat ac in erat. Donec vehicula pulvinar venenatis. Maecenas vehicula vehicula nibh. Nullam vehicula leo ex, sit amet consequat mi lacinia et. Cras aliquam 
            lacus nec turpis vestibulum feugiat. Suspendisse sit amet felis at arcu viverra ornare eu imperdiet arcu. Nullam pulvinar odio in nisi pharetra sodales. Fusce tempor ex in ligula rhoncus 
            ultrices. Cras enim elit, consectetur at vestibulum eget, laoreet sit amet erat. Integer id turpis urna.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

